{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bba5fe35b6bf8c58", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1772\n===\narXiv:2607.24430v1 [cs.HC] 27 Jul 2026\n\nLet Me Look at You: Advanced Facial Expression Modeling for\nConversational Speech Synthesis\nYifan Hu\n\nShuwei He\n\n32109235@mail.imu.edu.cn\nInner Mongolia University\nHohhot, China\n\nshuwei_he@163.com\nInner Mongolia University\nHohhot, China\n\nRui Liu✉\n\nHaizhou Li\n\nimucslr@imu.edu.cn\nInner Mongolia University\nHohhot, China\n\nhaizhouli@cuhk.edu.cn\nSRIBD, School of Artificial Intelligence\nThe Chinese University of Hong Kong\nShenzhen, China\n\nAbstract\n\nCCS Concepts\n\nConversational Speech Synthesis (CSS) is a fundamental component\nof human-computer interaction, aiming to generate contextually\nappropriate, expressive, and empathetic speech. However, facial\nexpressions encode subtle and rich affective cues that are crucial\nfor empathetic speech interaction, whereas existing approaches\noften overlook this important modality. In addition, the lack of\nlarge-scale natural conversational datasets with both speech and\nvisual modalities also limits the development of visual affect understanding in conversational settings. To address these limitations,\nwe propose FacialTalker, a facial-expression-aware CSS framework\nbuilt upon a large language model backbone. To efficiently encode\nfacial expressions, we propose AUTokenizer, a single-codebook\nvisual tokenizer that discretizes each frame-level facial expression\ninto a compact token, trained with supervision from combinations\nof facial Action Units. We further introduce a dual direct preference\noptimization (DualDPO) strategy, which extends the DPO by jointly\nimposing preference constraints on both visual and speech token\nsequences, to enhance the model’s understanding of facial expressions and speech semantics in multimodal conversational contexts.\nMoreover, we construct VSDD-1K, a large-scale multimodal dialogue dataset collected through a fully automated pipeline from\nreal-world Internet conversations, comprising over 1,033 hours of\nsynchronized speaker videos and speech, with more than 85% of\nframes containing valid faces. Extensive objective and subjective experiments demonstrate that FacialTalker consistently outperforms\nstrong baselines in facial-expression perception and speech synthesis quality, generating speech that is more natural, expressive,\nand better aligned with the conversational context. The results\nalso validate the effectiveness of our training strategy and dataset\nconstruction pipeline. The project page: https://github.com/walkerhyf/FacialTalker.\n\n• Information systems → Multimedia content creation.\n\nThis work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercialNoDerivatives 4.0 International License.\nMM ’26, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n© 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).\nACM ISBN 979-8-4007-2213-4/2026/11\nhttps://doi.org/10.1145/3767308.3835759\n\nKeywords\nConversational Speech Synthesis, User-agent Conversation, Facial\nExpression Modeling, Empathetic Speech\nACM Reference Format:\nYifan Hu, Shuwei He, Rui Liu, and Haizhou Li. 2026. Let Me Look at You:\nAdvanced Facial Expression Modeling for Conversational Speech Synthesis.\nIn Proceedings of the 34th ACM International Conference on Multimedia (MM\n’26), November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. ACM, New York, NY, USA,\n10 pages. https://doi.org/10.1145/3767308.3835759\n\n1\n\nIntroduction\n\nConversational speech synthesis (CSS) aims to generate empathetic\nspeech for the current user–agent interaction based on multimodal\ndialogue context [15]. Specifically, the synthesized speech should\nbe not only natural, but also appropriate in speaking style and\nemotional expression [33]. Despite recent advances in speech synthesis [7, 17, 71, 72], existing methods still struggle to provide\nsufficient emotional support in complex multi-turn dialogues. This\nlimitation is especially pronounced in practical applications such\nas smart homes [6], Smart Cockpit [59], and eldercare robots [57],\nwhere users increasingly expect spoken interactions to convey not\nonly information, but also richer emotional engagement.\nPrevious studies on CSS have improved speech naturalness and\nexpressiveness through various context modeling approaches. By\nexploiting multi-scale information from text and speech in dialogue\nhistory at the sentence and word levels, [18, 63] introduce both\ncoarse-grained and fine-grained contextual representations into the\nsynthesizer to enhance contextual understanding. However, such\nmethods often fail to capture complex contextual dependencies.\nTo address this limitation, [25, 33] employ graph neural networks\nto model dependencies by representing multimodal information\nin each utterance as nodes and speaker relations and cross-modal\ninteractions as edges, significantly improving contextual understanding. More recently, inspired by the autoregressive generation\nparadigm of large language models (LLMs), several studies [34, 44]\nfurther improve speech naturalness and emotional understanding with larger-scale training data. In parallel, LLM-based speech\ngeneration and spoken dialogue modeling have made remarkable\n\n\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nI’m not sure what facial expression\nthe user has or how they are feeling.\nDoes not support visual understanding\n\nMultimodal\nContext\nwithout Facial\nCues\n\nPrevious\nModel\n\n（a） Previous CSS Methods\n\nYifan Hu, Shuwei He, Rui Liu, and Haizhou Li.\n\nAlthough his tone is calm, he\nappears to look very happy.\nSupport facial expression modeling\n\nMultimodal\nContext\nwith Facial\nCues\n\nFacialTalker\n\n（b） Our Approach\n\nFigure 1: (a) Previous CSS methods lack visual perception\nability. (b) Our approach enables perception and modeling\nof the user’s facial expressions.\n\nprogress in scenarios such as natural speech generation and spoken\ninteraction [10, 31, 58, 62, 67], demonstrating the strong potential\nof token-based unified modeling for text and speech. Nevertheless,\nmost existing methods still primarily rely on textual and acoustic\ncontext, and thus remain limited in their ability to perceive users’\nnonverbal emotional signals.\nHowever, real-world interpersonal communication relies not\nonly on spoken language for information exchange and emotion\nexpression. Facial expressions, as a crucial source of nonverbal cues,\nplay an equally important role in conveying affective states [3, 23].\nIn particular, subtle micro-expressive changes in local facial regions\noften provide rich evidence for emotion perception [68]. As shown\nin Fig. 1, this modality remains largely underexplored in CSS due to\ntwo key challenges: (1) the lack of high-quality multimodal dialogue\ndatasets with synchronized visual and speech signals [5, 44], and (2)\nthe limited ability of existing visual encoders to capture subtle facial\nexpressions in conversational contexts [19, 69]. Therefore, how\ncan users’ facial expressions be effectively perceived and encoded\nduring contextual modeling for empathetic speech synthesis?\nIn this paper, we propose FacialTalker, a novel CSS system with\nfacial expression modeling. FacialTalker represents multimodal dialogue context, including speaker identity, text, speech, and face,\nas a unified sequence of discrete tokens for an LLM. Under the\nnext-token prediction paradigm, the model generates the emotion\ncategory and speech token sequence for the target utterance, which\nare then rendered into expressive empathetic speech. To better\ncapture facial affect, we develop AUTokenizer, a dedicated visual\ntokenizer that discretizes facial expressions into compact tokens\nunder supervision from combinations of facial Action Units (AUs).\nAUTokenizer is highly efficient, requiring only a single token per\nframe, and is well suited for LLM-based autoregressive modeling.\nWe further propose a dual direct preference optimization strategy\n(DualDPO) that extends DPO to jointly impose preference constraints on visual and speech token sequences, encouraging the\nLLM to attend to both facial expressions and speech semantics\nwithin multimodal context modeling. To address data scarcity, we\nconstruct VSDD-1K, a large-scale visual-speech dialogue dataset\nbuilt through a fully automatic pipeline, comprising approximately\n1,033 hours of data with comprehensive annotations, of which more\n\nTable 1: VSDD-1K vs. other conversational datasets. 𝑣 𝑠 and 𝑡\ndenote the visual, speech, and text modalities, respectively.\nDataset\nNCSSD [34]\nRAMC [65]\nDailyTalk [22]\nSTUDIES [52]\nRyanSpeech [66]\nCALLS [51]\nECC [24]\nMultiDialog [44]\nAvaMERG [69]\nVSDD-1K\n\nLanguage\nCN, EN\nCN\nEN\nJP\nEN\nJP\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "3abbfa66-0e8f-42c9-ba36-94f5ab07bf1d", "uuid": "314a92d2-c96c-4edc-bdf8-5327a298c695", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T05:41:50.940Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "1772\n===\narXiv:2607.24430v1 [cs.HC] 27 Jul 2026\n\nLet Me Look at You: Advanced Facial Expression Modeling for\nConversational Speech Synthesis\nYifan Hu\n\nShuwei He\n\n32109235@mail.imu.edu.cn\nInner Mongolia University\nHohhot, China\n\nshuwei_he@163.com\nInner Mongolia University\nHohhot, China\n\nRui Liu✉\n\nHaizhou Li\n\nimucslr@imu.edu.cn\nInner Mongolia University\nHohhot, China\n\nhaizhouli@cuhk.edu.cn\nSRIBD, School of Artificial Intelligence\nThe Chinese University of Hong Kong\nShenzhen, China\n\nAbstract\n\nCCS Concepts\n\nConversational Speech Synthesis (CSS) is a fundamental component\nof human-computer interaction, aiming to generate contextually\nappropriate, expressive, and empathetic speech. However, facial\nexpressions encode subtle and rich affective cues that are crucial\nfor empathetic speech interaction, whereas existing approaches\noften overlook this important modality. In addition, the lack of\nlarge-scale natural conversational datasets with both speech and\nvisual modalities also limits the development of visual affect understanding in conversational settings. To address these limitations,\nwe propose FacialTalker, a facial-expression-aware CSS framework\nbuilt upon a large language model backbone. To efficiently encode\nfacial expressions, we propose AUTokenizer, a single-codebook\nvisual tokenizer that discretizes each frame-level facial expression\ninto a compact token, trained with supervision from combinations\nof facial Action Units. We further introduce a dual direct preference\noptimization (DualDPO) strategy, which extends the DPO by jointly\nimposing preference constraints on both visual and speech token\nsequences, to enhance the model’s understanding of facial expressions and speech semantics in multimodal conversational contexts.\nMoreover, we construct VSDD-1K, a large-scale multimodal dialogue dataset collected through a fully automated pipeline from\nreal-world Internet conversations, comprising over 1,033 hours of\nsynchronized speaker videos and speech, with more than 85% of\nframes containing valid faces. Extensive objective and subjective experiments demonstrate that FacialTalker consistently outperforms\nstrong baselines in facial-expression perception and speech synthesis quality, generating speech that is more natural, expressive,\nand better aligned with the conversational context. The results\nalso validate the effectiveness of our training strategy and dataset\nconstruction pipeline. The project page: https://github.com/walkerhyf/FacialTalker.\n\n• Information systems → Multimedia content creation.\n\nThis work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercialNoDerivatives 4.0 International License.\nMM ’26, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n© 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).\nACM ISBN 979-8-4007-2213-4/2026/11\nhttps://doi.org/10.1145/3767308.3835759\n\nKeywords\nConversational Speech Synthesis, User-agent Conversation, Facial\nExpression Modeling, Empathetic Speech\nACM Reference Format:\nYifan Hu, Shuwei He, Rui Liu, and Haizhou Li. 2026. Let Me Look at You:\nAdvanced Facial Expression Modeling for Conversational Speech Synthesis.\nIn Proceedings of the 34th ACM International Conference on Multimedia (MM\n’26), November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. ACM, New York, NY, USA,\n10 pages. https://doi.org/10.1145/3767308.3835759\n\n1\n\nIntroduction\n\nConversational speech synthesis (CSS) aims to generate empathetic\nspeech for the current user–agent interaction based on multimodal\ndialogue context [15]. Specifically, the synthesized speech should\nbe not only natural, but also appropriate in speaking style and\nemotional expression [33]. Despite recent advances in speech synthesis [7, 17, 71, 72], existing methods still struggle to provide\nsufficient emotional support in complex multi-turn dialogues. This\nlimitation is especially pronounced in practical applications such\nas smart homes [6], Smart Cockpit [59], and eldercare robots [57],\nwhere users increasingly expect spoken interactions to convey not\nonly information, but also richer emotional engagement.\nPrevious studies on CSS have improved speech naturalness and\nexpressiveness through various context modeling approaches. By\nexploiting multi-scale information from text and speech in dialogue\nhistory at the sentence and word levels, [18, 63] introduce both\ncoarse-grained and fine-grained contextual representations into the\nsynthesizer to enhance contextual understanding. However, such\nmethods often fail to capture complex contextual dependencies.\nTo address this limitation, [25, 33] employ graph neural networks\nto model dependencies by representing multimodal information\nin each utterance as nodes and speaker relations and cross-modal\ninteractions as edges, significantly improving contextual understanding. More recently, inspired by the autoregressive generation\nparadigm of large language models (LLMs), several studies [34, 44]\nfurther improve speech naturalness and emotional understanding with larger-scale training data. In parallel, LLM-based speech\ngeneration and spoken dialogue modeling have made remarkable\n\n\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nI’m not sure what facial expression\nthe user has or how they are feeling.\nDoes not support visual understanding\n\nMultimodal\nContext\nwithout Facial\nCues\n\nPrevious\nModel\n\n（a） Previous CSS Methods\n\nYifan Hu, Shuwei He, Rui Liu, and Haizhou Li.\n\nAlthough his tone is calm, he\nappears to look very happy.\nSupport facial expression modeling\n\nMultimodal\nContext\nwith Facial\nCues\n\nFacialTalker\n\n（b） Our Approach\n\nFigure 1: (a) Previous CSS methods lack visual perception\nability. (b) Our approach enables perception and modeling\nof the user’s facial expressions.\n\nprogress in scenarios such as natural speech generation and spoken\ninteraction [10, 31, 58, 62, 67], demonstrating the strong potential\nof token-based unified modeling for text and speech. Nevertheless,\nmost existing methods still primarily rely on textual and acoustic\ncontext, and thus remain limited in their ability to perceive users’\nnonverbal emotional signals.\nHowever, real-world interpersonal communication relies not\nonly on spoken language for information exchange and emotion\nexpression. Facial expressions, as a crucial source of nonverbal cues,\nplay an equally important role in conveying affective states [3, 23].\nIn particular, subtle micro-expressive changes in local facial regions\noften provide rich evidence for emotion perception [68]. As shown\nin Fig. 1, this modality remains largely underexplored in CSS due to\ntwo key challenges: (1) the lack of high-quality multimodal dialogue\ndatasets with synchronized visual and speech signals [5, 44], and (2)\nthe limited ability of existing visual encoders to capture subtle facial\nexpressions in conversational contexts [19, 69]. Therefore, how\ncan users’ facial expressions be effectively perceived and encoded\nduring contextual modeling for empathetic speech synthesis?\nIn this paper, we propose FacialTalker, a novel CSS system with\nfacial expression modeling. FacialTalker represents multimodal dialogue context, including speaker identity, text, speech, and face,\nas a unified sequence of discrete tokens for an LLM. Under the\nnext-token prediction paradigm, the model generates the emotion\ncategory and speech token sequence for the target utterance, which\nare then rendered into expressive empathetic speech. To better\ncapture facial affect, we develop AUTokenizer, a dedicated visual\ntokenizer that discretizes facial expressions into compact tokens\nunder supervision from combinations of facial Action Units (AUs).\nAUTokenizer is highly efficient, requiring only a single token per\nframe, and is well suited for LLM-based autoregressive modeling.\nWe further propose a dual direct preference optimization strategy\n(DualDPO) that extends DPO to jointly impose preference constraints on visual and speech token sequences, encouraging the\nLLM to attend to both facial expressions and speech semantics\nwithin multimodal context modeling. To address data scarcity, we\nconstruct VSDD-1K, a large-scale visual-speech dialogue dataset\nbuilt through a fully automatic pipeline, comprising approximately\n1,033 hours of data with comprehensive annotations, of which more\n\nTable 1: VSDD-1K vs. other conversational datasets. 𝑣 𝑠 and 𝑡\ndenote the visual, speech, and text modalities, respectively.\nDataset\nNCSSD [34]\nRAMC [65]\nDailyTalk [22]\nSTUDIES [52]\nRyanSpeech [66]\nCALLS [51]\nECC [24]\nMultiDialog [44]\nAvaMERG [69]\nVSDD-1K\n\nLanguage\nCN, EN\nCN\nEN\nJP\nEN\nJP", "stderr": "\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "interrupted": false, "isI