{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8cf6a4de5fce3df2", "type": "tool_result", "content": "JP\nEN\nEN\nEN\nEN\n\nModal\n( ,a,t)\n( ,a,t)\n( ,a,t)\n( ,a,t)\n( ,a,t)\n( ,a,t)\n(v,a,t)\n(v,a,t)\n(v,a,t)\n(v,a,t)\n\nDuration (h)\n236\n180\n20\n8.2\n10\n6.5\n24\n340\n239\n1033\n\nSpeaker\n>776\n663\n2\n3\n1\n1\n> 26\n12\n65\n>1498\n\nthan 85% of the video frames contain valid faces. Extensive objective and subjective evaluations demonstrate the effectiveness of\nVSDD-1K, the reliability of AUTokenizer, and the superiority of\nFacialTalker for empathetic speech synthesis.\nIn summary, the main contributions of this paper are as follows:\n• Model: We propose FacialTalker, a novel CSS model that\nincorporates facial affect modeling. We further develop AUTokenizer, an efficient visual tokenizer that encodes facial\nexpression information into a single token per frame.\n• Training strategy: We introduce a dual direct preference optimization strategy during post-training to further enhance\nthe model’s ability to jointly model facial expressions and\nspeech semantics.\n• Dataset: We develop a fully automatic pipeline for videospeech dialogue processing and construct VSDD-1K, a largescale open-source video-speech dialogue dataset comprising\napproximately 1K hours of speech with well-aligned facial\nimages for multimodal modeling.\n• Experiments: Extensive objective and subjective experiments demonstrate the reliability and effectiveness of our\nmodel, training strategy, and dataset.\n\n2 Related Work\n2.1 Multimodal Dialogue Dataset with Visual\nSignal\nOpen-source multimodal multi-party dialogue datasets provide an\nimportant foundation for research on dialogue understanding and\ngeneration. For example, IEMOCAP [5], MELD [45], CPED [9], and\nM3 ED [70] support tasks such as conversational emotion recognition and dialogue act classification. However, their speech data often\ncontain substantial noise and exhibit relatively low audio quality,\nwhich limits their value for training high-quality speech synthesis\nmodels. To the best of our knowledge, Table 1 summarizes currently\navailable representative open-source datasets for target dialogue\nspeech synthesis. NCSSD [34] uses a combination of data collection\nand manual recording to construct 236 hours of conversational\nspeech synthesis data, including 188 hours in the collected subset\nand 48 hours in the recorded subset. RAMC [65] contains about 180\nhours of speech manually recorded with smartphones in indoor\nenvironments. DailyTalk [22] builds on DailyDialog [29] scripts and\nprovides about 20 hours of speech recorded by one male speaker\n\n\fLet Me Look at You: Advanced Facial Expression Modeling for Conversational Speech Synthesis\n\n(1) Multimodal Conversational Context\n\n😟\n🤗\n\nConversational History\nI feel a little stressed today.\n\nTarget Utterance\nI’m glad to hear that. Keep\ngoing, you’ve got this.\n\n😃\n\nTake a deep breath. You’re\ndoing better than you think.\n\nThank you, that\nmakes me feel\nbetter.\n\n😊\n\nMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\n(4) Empathetic Speech Rendering\nFacial Tokens\nchosen\nrejected\n\nSpeech Tokens\nchosen\n\nrejected\nSpeech Synthesizer\n\nDual Direct Preference Optimization (only training)\nSpeech\n\nTimbre\n\n...\n\nVideo\n\nT\n\nE\n\nLLM (Autoregressive Modeling)\nSpeaker\n\nSpeaker\nEncoder\n\nSpeech\n\nUser face\n\nAUTokenizer\n\n<EMO>\n\nText\n\nText\nTokenizer\n\n<BOS>\n\nSpeech\nTokenizer\n\nB\n\n...\n\nB\n\n<TASK>\n\nT\n\n<EOS>\n\nE\n\nT\n\n...\n\nConversational History\n\n(2) Multimodal Conversational Context Tokenization\n\nTarget Utterance\n\n(3) Multimodal Dialogue Context Modeling\n\nFigure 2: The overview of FacialTalker. The framework consists of four main parts: (1) Multimodal Conversational Context in\nthe user-agent interaction, (2) multimodal information (H, C) tokenization via Multimodal Conversational Context Tokenization,\n′\n(3) feeding the serialized dialogue sequence into Multimodal Dialogue Context Modeling to predict emotion categorie 𝑇𝑁𝑒 and\n′\nspeech tokens 𝑇𝑁𝑠 ,1→𝐿 , with Dual Direct Preference Optimization further enhancing visual and speech perception and modeling\n′\nduring training, and (4) Empathetic Speech Rendering (𝑈 𝑁𝑠 ) for the user.\nand one female speaker. STUDIES [52] records teacher-student\ninteractions and contains 8.2 hours of speech. RyanSpeech [66]\nand CALLS [51] contain 10 and 6.5 hours of conversational speech,\nrespectively, but only preserve speech from one participant. Moreover, these datasets do not include visual signals and only provide\nspeech and transcripts. ECC [24] collects about 66 English dialogue\nvideos from YouTube, but it does not further process the videos\nfor tasks such as target speaker extraction. MultiDialog [44] and\nAvaMERG [69] provide manually recorded dialogue videos, but\ntheir speakers talk to the camera rather than engage in direct faceto-face interaction, which makes the conversations less realistic and\nnatural. In contrast, VSDD-1K consists entirely of real-world faceto-face conversations collected from scenarios such as interviews\nand podcasts. As a result, it better reflects natural facial expressions\nduring conversations.\n\n2.2\n\nFacial Encoding in Multimodal Dialogue\nModeling\n\nVisual encoders play a central role in computer vision because\nthey directly affect how well a model understands visual information [43]. Although recent spoken dialogue systems [54, 61] and\nomni-modal models [1, 60] incorporate visual input, they mainly\nfocus on scenes, objects, and other general visual content rather\nthan user facial expressions. Some spoken dialogue models begin\nto consider facial information. For example, Empatheia [69], EmpathyEar [13], and Mini-Omni2 [60] use visual encoders such as\nCLIP [46] for facial expression perception. However, these generalpurpose pretrained models mainly optimize high-dimensional imagetext alignment, which often leads to insufficient modeling of local facial details and unnecessary attention to non-facial regions.\nUniTalker [19] further explores facial expression modeling through\n\nfacial landmark encoding. However, it uses only 128 landmarks and\ncovers only the mouth, eyes, and eyebrows, which limits its ability\nto capture muscle movements from other facial regions. Unlike\nprevious work, we represent facial expressions using facial Action\nUnits. We adopt AUs because individual units capture subtle local\nfacial muscle movements, while combinations of multiple AUs provide a structured description of overall facial expressions [39]. To\nbetter interface with LLMs, we design AUTokenizer to quantize\nfacial expression information into discrete tokens, trained with supervision from AU combination labels. In contrast to general visual\ntokenizers [12, 56], which often require at least 64 × 64 tokens to\nreconstruct an image or video frame, AUTokenizer leverages FSQ\nto quantize each facial frame into a single token while preserving\nrich facial expression information.\n\n3\n\nTask Definition\n\nThe goal is to enable the model to understand the user’s facial expressions 𝑈𝑖𝑣 and speech 𝑈𝑖𝑠 through contextual modeling, and then\n′\n′\ngenerate an appropriate target emotion 𝑈 𝑁𝑒 and target speech 𝑈 𝑁𝑠 .\n′\n𝑠\nIn particular, 𝑈 𝑁 should not only natural and remain consistent\n′\nwith 𝑈 𝑁𝑒 , but also match the style, timbre, and emotion of the overall\ndialogue context. In CSS, the dialogue context typically consists of\ntwo parts: the dialogue history H and the target utterance C. The\n𝑁 −1 .\ndialogue history H contains 𝑁 − 1 utterances, denoted as {𝑈𝑖 }𝑖=1\n𝑝\nEach utterance 𝑈𝑖 is represented as a five-tuple (𝑈𝑖 , 𝑈𝑖𝑣 , 𝑈𝑖𝑡 , 𝑈𝑖𝑒 , 𝑈𝑖𝑠 ),\n𝑝\nwhere 𝑈𝑖 , 𝑈𝑖𝑣 , 𝑈𝑖𝑡 , 𝑈𝑖𝑒 , and 𝑈𝑖𝑠 denote the speaker identity, facial\nexpression, text, emotion label, and speech, respectively, and 𝑖 denotes the dialogue turn. Notably, we only include the facial visual\nmodality 𝑈𝑖𝑣 for user turns, which better reflects real-world application scenarios. The target utterance C consists of the agent identity\n\n\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nFacial Token\n\nDropout2d\n\n1×1 Conv\n\nGELU\n\nEncoder\nQuery\n\n3×3 Depthwise Conv\n\nLayerNorm2d\n\nSoftmax\n\n1×1 Conv\n\nLayerNorm2d\n\nStage_1\nStage_2\n(96x56x56) (192x28x28)\n\nFSQ_down\n\nFSQ_up\n\nRound\n\nDecoder\n\ne.g. AU12, AU4\nCombination of Facial\nAction Unit Labels\n\nLevels = [8,8,8,8,8]\n\nAUPredictor\n\nAUTokenizer\n\nDecoder\nConcatenate\n\nLinear\n\nDropout\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "3abbfa66-0e8f-42c9-ba36-94f5ab07bf1d", "uuid": "e13bb838-021b-407a-8127-87f8fa3b89d8", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T05:42:01.649Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "JP\nEN\nEN\nEN\nEN\n\nModal\n( ,a,t)\n( ,a,t)\n( ,a,t)\n( ,a,t)\n( ,a,t)\n( ,a,t)\n(v,a,t)\n(v,a,t)\n(v,a,t)\n(v,a,t)\n\nDuration (h)\n236\n180\n20\n8.2\n10\n6.5\n24\n340\n239\n1033\n\nSpeaker\n>776\n663\n2\n3\n1\n1\n> 26\n12\n65\n>1498\n\nthan 85% of the video frames contain valid faces. Extensive objective and subjective evaluations demonstrate the effectiveness of\nVSDD-1K, the reliability of AUTokenizer, and the superiority of\nFacialTalker for empathetic speech synthesis.\nIn summary, the main contributions of this paper are as follows:\n• Model: We propose FacialTalker, a novel CSS model that\nincorporates facial affect modeling. We further develop AUTokenizer, an efficient visual tokenizer that encodes facial\nexpression information into a single token per frame.\n• Training strategy: We introduce a dual direct preference optimization strategy during post-training to further enhance\nthe model’s ability to jointly model facial expressions and\nspeech semantics.\n• Dataset: We develop a fully automatic pipeline for videospeech dialogue processing and construct VSDD-1K, a larg