{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-902e645b1fd32b04", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# CosyVoice 2: Scalable Streaming Speech Synthesis with Large Language Models\narXiv: 2412.10117\nAuthors: Zhihao Du, Yuxuan Wang, Qian Chen, Xian Shi, Xiang Lv, Tianyu Zhao, Zhifu Gao, Yexin Yang, Changfeng Gao, Hui Wang, Fan Yu, Huadai Liu, Zhengyan Sheng, Yue Gu, Chong Deng, Wen Wang, Shiliang Zhang, Zhijie Yan, Jingren Zhou\nYear: 2024 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introducing CosyVoice 2, a scalable streaming speech synthesis model that integrates large language models with a speech decoder.\n  2. Designing a unified architecture that seamlessly supports both streaming and non-streaming speech synthesis.\n  3. Developing a large language model equipped with block-wise causal attention to effectively balance lookahead context and streaming latency.\n  4. Implementing a modified finite scalar quantization strategy with an adaptive weight to achieve high-compression and high-accuracy speech tokenization.\n  5. Demonstrating through comprehensive experiments that the proposed model significantly outperforms its predecessor and achieves state-of-the-art performance in speech synthesis.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-AR-LLM, TTS/功能-流式, TTS/功能-多语言, TTS/控制-参考语音\n===\n# BridgeCode: A Dual Speech Representation Paradigm for Autoregressive Zero-Shot Text-to-Speech Synthesis\narXiv: 2510.11646\nAuthors: Jingyuan Xing, Mingru Yang, Zhipeng Li, Xiaofen Xing, Xiangmin Xu\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We propose BridgeCode, a dual speech representation paradigm that incorporates sparse tokens and dense continuous features, along with two trained bridging modules for bidirectional conversion between them.\n  2. Building on BridgeCode, we introduce the BridgeTTS framework, which substantially reduces AR prediction steps without compromising synthesis quality.\n  3. Experimental results demonstrate that BridgeTTS attains the lowest AR token rate among existing methods while achieving competitive naturalness and speaker similarity, and effectively mitigating error accumulation while accelerating synthesis speed.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-AR-LLM, TTS/功能-快速生成, 压缩/量化-RVQ\n===\n# WavChat: A Survey of Spoken Dialogue Models\narXiv: 2411.13577\nAuthors: Shengpeng Ji, Yifu Chen, Minghui Fang, Jialong Zuo, Jingyu Lu, Hanting Wang, Ziyue Jiang, Long Zhou, Shujie Liu, Xize Cheng, Xiaoda Yang, Zehan Wang, Qian Yang, Jian Li, Yidi Jiang, Jingzhen He, Yunfei Chu, Jin Xu, Zhou Zhao\nYear: 2024 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Categorization of existing spoken dialogue systems into cascaded and end-to-end paradigms based on whether the core language model directly understands and generates speech representations.\n  2. In-depth overview of core technologies including speech representation, training paradigms, streaming capabilities, and duplex interaction mechanisms.\n  3. Discussion of the limitations of current speech representations and training paradigms, outlining considerations for future research in these areas.\n  4. Thorough review of relevant training datasets, evaluation metrics, and benchmarks from the perspectives of training and evaluating spoken dialogue systems.\n\nTags: LALM, LALM/对话型\n===\n# SoulX-Podcast: Towards Realistic Long-form Podcasts with Dialectal and Paralinguistic Diversity\narXiv: 2510.23541\nAuthors: Hanke Xie, Haopeng Lin, Wenxiao Cao, Dake Guo, Wenjie Tian, Jun Wu, Hanlin Wen, Ruixuan Shang, Hongmei Liu, Zhiqi Jiang, Yuepeng Jiang, Wenxi Chen, Ruiqi Yan, Jiale Qian, Yichao Yan, Shunshun Yin, Ming Tao, Xie Chen, Lei Xie, Xinsheng Wang\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduces SoulX-Podcast, a large language model-driven framework for long-form, multi-speaker, and multi-dialect podcast-style conversational speech synthesis.\n  2. Proposes an interleaved text-speech token organization that chronologically aligns speaker-labeled text and speech tokens to facilitate coherent multi-turn dialogue generation.\n  3. Integrates fine-grained paralinguistic controls to generate expressive nonverbal cues such as laughter, sighs, and coughs within conversations.\n  4. Supports multiple Chinese dialects, including Sichuanese, Henanese, and Cantonese, with cross-dialectal zero-shot voice cloning capabilities.\n  5. Introduces a Dialect-Guided Prompting inference strategy to effectively steer speech generation toward target dialectal characteristics using a Mandarin prompt.\n  6. Employs a curriculum learning strategy and a context regularization mechanism to enhance long-form dialogue coherence and stability.\n  7. Achieves state-of-the-art performance in both multi-turn dialogue synthesis and conventional zero-shot monologue text-to-speech tasks.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-AR-LLM, TTS/功能-多说话人, TTS/功能-方言小语种, TTS/功能-长时间, TTS/控制-风格标签", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "3abbfa66-0e8f-42c9-ba36-94f5ab07bf1d", "uuid": "5c245738-f4b0-4399-b7a0-c23302a21fb9", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T05:44:22.538Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# CosyVoice 2: Scalable Streaming Speech Synthesis with Large Language Models\narXiv: 2412.10117\nAuthors: Zhihao Du, Yuxuan Wang, Qian Chen, Xian Shi, Xiang Lv, Tianyu Zhao, Zhifu Gao, Yexin Yang, Changfeng Gao, Hui Wang, Fan Yu, Huadai Liu, Zhengyan Sheng, Yue Gu, Chong Deng, Wen Wang, Shiliang Zhang, Zhijie Yan, Jingren Zhou\nYear: 2024 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introducing CosyVoice 2, a scalable streaming speech synthesis model that integrates large language models with a speech decoder.\n  2. Designing a unified architecture that seamlessly supports both streaming and non-streaming speech synthesis.\n  3. Developing a large language model equipped with block-wise causal attention to effectively balance lookahead context and streaming latency.\n  4. Implementing a modified finite scalar quantization strategy with an adaptive weight to achieve high-compression and high-accuracy speech tokenization.\n  5. Demonstrating through comprehensive experiments that the proposed model significantly outperforms its predecessor and achieves state-of-the-art performance in speech synthesis.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-AR-LLM, TTS/功能-流式, TTS/功能-多语言, TTS/控制-参考语音\n===\n# BridgeCode: A Dual Speech Representation Paradigm for Autoregressive Zero-Shot Text-to-Speech Synthesis\narXiv: 2510.11646\nAuthors: Jingyuan Xing, Mingru Yang, Zhipeng Li, Xiaofen Xing, Xiangmin Xu\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We propose BridgeCode, a dual speech representation paradigm that incorporates sparse tokens and dense continuous features, along with two trained bridging modules for bidirectional conversion between them.\n  2. Building on BridgeCode, we introduce the BridgeTTS framework, which substantially reduces AR prediction steps without compromising synthesis quality.\n  3. Experimental results demonstrate that BridgeTTS attains the lowest AR token rate among existing methods while achieving competitive naturalness and speaker similarity, and effectively mitigating error accumulation while accelerating synthesis speed.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-AR-LLM, TTS/功能-快速生成, 压缩/量化-RVQ\n===\n# WavChat: A Survey of Spoken Dialogue Models\narXiv: 2411.13577\nAuthors: Shengpeng Ji, Yifu Chen, Minghui Fang, Jialong Zuo, Jingyu Lu, Hanting Wang, Ziyue Jiang, Long Zhou, Shujie Liu, Xize Cheng, Xiaoda Yang, Zehan Wang, Qian Yang, Jian Li, Yidi Jiang, Jingzhen He, Yunfei Chu, Jin Xu, Zhou Zhao\nYear: 2024 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Categorization of existing spoken dialogue systems into cascaded and end-to-end paradigms based on whether the core language model directly understands and generates speech representations.\n  2. In-depth overview of core technologies including speech representation, t